content.text:
Second run failed with TimeoutError again, likely intermittent. Need full output. It seems auto-start sometimes fails because of actionability/stability, regardless of our animation? The trial/click waits. Let's investigate the failure error. It printed stack tail. Run again capturing full output. Also note that previous run at 6.68s failed once too, then succeeded. Intermittency suggests the page game loop/main thread busy (SwiftShader) causes Playwright actionability checks to miss two consecutive stable frames within timeout. At 1440x900 fps 2 means each frame 500ms; actionability requires the element box stable across rAF frames maybe; with 2fps it should still work within 2s, but the button's box is static.

Playwright click waits for "element to be visible, enabled and stable"; stability is computed based on two consecutive rAF with same box. At 2fps, two frames = 1s, within 2s. But under heavy load, maybe rAFs take >1s and timeout. The title/menu animation (logo glow filter, etc.) consumes CPU/GPU. Could disable background canvas rendering while in menu? The game still renders every frame even at menu, painting rain and city at 2fps. This makes the page extremely slow in software, causing click actionability timeouts. If we reduce menu rendering workload / pause rAF when menu is static except minimal, clicks become reliable.

Options:
1. In menu, render at lower frequency or stop continuous rendering after initial frames. But menu has animated rain/actors; we can throttle to e.g. 20fps, but under 2fps that's not the issue. Actually rendering at 1440x900 every frame is what makes the main thread busy/rAF slow. Skip rendering every other frame in menu, or reduce canvas resolution in menu (we saw lower res improves fps). For actionability, we need rAF callbacks frequent. Could stop the render loop entirely for 1s after menu? But the click's stability check relies on rAF; if no rAF, Playwright's checks may still use its own raf? It runs in the page and likely requires animation frames. Stopping rAF could worsen.

2. Optimize menu rendering so fps improves: menu currently draws full world + 3 actors + rain + atmosphere at 1440x900. Could reduce by rendering at a lower internal resolution for menu only, or cache the entire menu scene as a static image and only animate subtle elements. But our background is already mostly static; the expensive part is drawing large images and gradients each frame. Caching the composed scene to an offscreen canvas and blitting once would help. We could render the full scene (sky+city+ground+atmosphere) into a cached canvas, then blit that each frame instead of multiple big images and gradients. For gameplay the camera moves and layering is complex, but for menu, camera barely drifts. Could cache at current cam and redraw when cam changes >1px; menu cam oscillates slowly, so would still redraw often.

3. Simply remove the menu animation and pause the render loop once the menu settles, until user interaction. If we stop rAF after e.g. 60 frames in menu, the canvas freezes (fine, title screen static), and actionability rAF? Playwright might still fire rAF when needed; but if our loop stops, no more frames. The browser still generates vsync frames even without callbacks? Playwright's stability check uses `requestAnimationFrame` in page; it will get callbacks regardless of our loop. Since our loop would stop, main thread free => 60fps rAF, click succeeds. This is promising! And we can resume the loop on pointermove/keypress/start. Actually just keep a low-frequency timeout for menu animations? We could stop rendering menu entirely after initial scene is drawn, and restart loop when state changes. The title screen can be static; the design still has CSS animations (logo glow, etc.) and the stickmen frozen in a pose. That's acceptable, though losing the ambient rain/animation. Better: throttle menu rendering to 8fps via setTimeout, which frees the main thread between frames; Playwright actionability may still have 2 consecutive frames? It uses rAF; between renders, rAF callbacks still fire at 60fps because the main thread is idle! Great. The issue is our game loop hogs the main thread at 2fps; if we render menu only every 125ms, the browser main thread has idle time, and rAF checks occur at 60fps. 

But is render synchronous and blocking 500ms each at 1440? It must be, since JS measured 1ms but raster might block compositor. The main thread maybe waiting on canvas GPU sync. Throttling would free it.

Simplest robust fix: while in menu, render at most every 100ms (10fps) using a time check in main frame; still call requestAnimationFrame every frame (rAF loop), but skip game.render on most frames. That keeps rAF ticking at whatever rate and frees main thread. Similarly during paused/gameover/victory overlays, skip rendering updates? They are static backgrounds but particles animate slightly. For click reliability on overlays, also throttle.

Also skip `game.update`? Menu update is cheap, keep it for actors. Only render every 120ms. The title background will animate at ~8fps, acceptable, and CSS animations stay smooth.

But the click happens while menu looping. With our rAF still running every frame and render skipped, main thread idle -> Playwright checks fast. Test.

Implement in main.js:
```
let lastRender = 0;
function frame(now) {
 ...
 game.update(dt);
 const idle = game.state==='menu' || game.state==='paused' || ...;
 if (!idle || now - lastRender > 120 || dirty) { game.render(); lastRender=now; }
 ...
}
```
But on state transition to playing, render immediately. Dirty detection: if game.state !== lastState, render.

Also when menu actions attack, poses change over time; 8fps is fine.

However, the screenshot at capture needs current render; last render within 120ms, fine.

Also the CSS overlay animation (title fade) is compositor-driven, smooth.

Let's implement menu throttle. We can apply to menu only, because gameplay auto-start after click no longer needs clicks (except overlays). For gameover/victory overlay buttons, Playwright may click them too; main thread still renders gameplay at 2fps? In gameover/victory state, game.update returns early and render still draws full scene (with particles), so overlay buttons may also be unreliable. We can throttle rendering in any non-playing state. Use a `shouldThrottle` for menu/paused/gameover/victory, 100ms.

Actually during 'paused' we still want the frozen scene; no need to re-render at all. We can render one frame on state change, then stop rendering until state change. But particles update in paused (game.update updates particles at 0.2x), so slight motion. Not important. For gameover/victory, particles from death continue; but static is fine. Better to keep 8fps to animate them.

Let's implement:
```
let lastRenderAt = 0;
let lastState = '';
function frame(now) {
 ...
 game.update(dt);
 const calm = game.state !== 'playing' && game.state !== 'dying';
 if (!calm || now - lastRenderAt > 110 || game.state !== lastState) {
   game.render();
   lastRenderAt = now;
   lastState = game.state;
 }
 ...
}
```
Wait if calm and render throttled, the world.update still advances rain, but background image drawn at 8fps. Fine.

But careful: when starting from menu, capture clicks, startGame sets state playing, next frame renders immediately due to state change. Good. When clicking pause, state paused -> render once then 8fps.

Let's edit.

